= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Apply the <Runge-Kutta method> to the <Dahlquist test equation> and put $z=h\lambda$. Its <stability function> is
$$
R(z)=1+z\,b^T(I-zA)^{-1}\mathbf1=\frac{P(z)}{Q(z)},\qquad Q(z)=\det(I-zA).
$$
Since $A$ is an <invertible matrix>, $Q$ has <polynomial degree> $s$. The <adjugate matrix> formula gives $\deg P\le s$, but the additional condition gives
$$
\lim_{z\to\infty}R(z)=1-b^TA^{-1}\mathbf1=0,
$$
so actually $\deg P\le s-1$. The specified <order of a numerical method> implies $R(z)=e^z+O(z^{2s})$ near zero. Therefore the <Padé identification of a Radau stability function> makes $R$ the $[s-1/s]$ <Padé approximant> to the <exponential function>: its numerator and denominator have the required degrees, and its <Taylor series> agrees through degree $2s-1$.

In the permitted <A-stability of near-diagonal exponential Padé approximants>, take $m=s-1$, $n=s$. Then $n-2\le m\le n$, so **the scheme is <A-stable>**. The same <stability function> also tends to zero at infinity, giving <L-stability>.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For one stage, first-order <consistency of a numerical method> gives $b_1=1$. The condition $b^TA^{-1}\mathbf1=1$ then gives $a_{11}=1$. Hence the scheme is **the <backward Euler method>**:
$$
y_{n+1}=y_n+h f(t_n+h,y_{n+1}).
$$
It is a one-node <collocation Runge-Kutta method> with $c_1=1$. Specifically, choose a linear <polynomial> $p(t)$ on $[t_n,t_n+h]$ with $p(t_n)=y_n$ and impose $p'(t_n+h)=f(t_n+h,p(t_n+h))$. Its derivative is constant, so $p(t_n+h)-p(t_n)=hp'(t_n+h)$, exactly the <backward Euler method> update.

= 2
{parent=Paper 69}
